int l = 0;
int r = n - 1;

while (l < r) {
  int sum = arr[l] + arr[r];
  if (sum == target) return true;
  else if (sum < target) l++;
  else r--;
}

return false;
```

Note: Theoretical risk of overflow with `arr[l] + arr[r]`.

```c
int l = 0;
int r = n - 1;

// Initialize to first candidate
int best_l = l;
int best_r = r;
int best_diff = abs(arr[l] + arr[r] - target);

while (l < r) {
  int sum = arr[l] + arr[r];

  if (sum == target) break;  // exact match

  int diff = abs(sum - target);

  if (diff < best_diff) {
    best_diff = diff;
    best_l = l;
    best_r = r;
  }

  if (sum < target) l++;
  else r--;  // i.e. `if (sum > target)`, since == has been tested already
}
```

Comments:

- Initializes `best_l` and `best_r` to first candidate: this makes for redundant evaluation of the first candidate during the first loop iteration in exchange for avoiding an `INT_MAX` sentinel
- Theoretical risk of overflow with `arr[l] + arr[r]` and underflow *or* overflow with `sum - target`.

