```c
int l = 0;
int r = n - 1;

int best_l = l;
int best_r = r;
int best_area = 0;  // guaranteed minimum because `height` is nonnegative

while (l < r) {
  int area = min(height[l], height[r]) * (r - l);

  if (area > best_area) {
    best_l = l;
    best_r = r;
    best_area = area;
  }

  if (height[l] < height[r]) l++;
  else r--;
}
```

Idiom: after evaluating the current pair of heights, move the pointer at the shorter side.

The algorithm's idea: assuming `h(l) <= h(r)`, then  `area(r', l) <= area(r, l)` for all `r' < r`. Thus moving `r` inward can never improve area, and so `l` should be moved instead.

Note: this is an artificial Leetcode problem.
